    "result_text": "Addresses are now normalized whenever an owner is created. The test suite passes: all 3 acceptance tests, with no failures or errors. Those 3 tests are the whole suite in this repo, so nothing else covers owner creation.\n\n**What changed**\n- **New `util/AddressNormalizer.java`:** trims the address, collapses runs of whitespace, upper-cases it, and expands whole-word `ST`\u2192`STREET`, `RD`\u2192`ROAD` and `AVE`\u2192`AVENUE`. For example, `\"  12  main  st \"` becomes `\"12 MAIN STREET\"`. I put it in `util` rather than `rest.validation` so the service layer can use it without depending on the REST package.\n- **`RequiredOwnerFields`:** checks the normalized address, so one that is blank after normalization is rejected with 400. This also applies to the error report built in `ExceptionControllerAdvice`.\n- **`OwnerRestControllerV1.addOwner`:** normalizes the address once and uses that value for what gets stored and returned, the duplicate-household check, and the `DuplicateHouseholdException` payload.\n- **`ClinicServiceImpl.findHouseholdMembers`:** both duplicate detection and the shared household id now compare normalized addresses. Existing owners' addresses are normalized at comparison time, so rows saved before this change, such as the seed data, still match.\n\n**Things you might not expect**\n- **Trailing periods:** an abbreviation followed by a period (`St.`, `Ave.`) is also expanded. The spec didn't say either way; I added it because the seed data uses that style.\n- **Whole words only:** expansion only applies to complete words, so `STATE` is left alone. But a leading `ST` meaning \"Saint\" will also become `STREET`.\n- **Updates are not normalized:** `updateOwner` still stores the address exactly as sent, because the spec only covers creation. Say if you want updates normalized too.",
